## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constant-time, multi-word popcount without branches. Bridges knowledge of SWAR patterns from 64-bit into 128-bit by handling halves separately. It also checks handling of non-power-of-two widths.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR/popcount on 64-bit
- 128-bit word handling (hi/lo)
- Branch-free, mask-shift arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t countSetBits64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return countSetBits64(x.lo) + countSetBits64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values?
- How to verify edge cases (all-ones, zero) across both halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

